          If protein shakes leave you bloated, gassy or running for the bathroom, the protein is usually the last thing to blame. The common culprits are lactose in whey concentrate, sugar alcohols and chicory root fiber added to cut calories, a few thickening gums, and servings that are simply bigger than your gut wants at one time. The best protein powder for a sensitive stomach is therefore a short, boring label: an isolate or hydrolysate, or a certified low-FODMAP option, with nothing on it that you have not chosen. Here are six that fit, and how to work out which ingredient is actually bothering you.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-it-happens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Protein Powder Upsets Some Stomachs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A protein shake is a strange meal from your gut's point of view. It delivers 25 to 50 g of protein, plus whatever else the manufacturer added, in a few hundred milliliters of liquid that you probably drink in under a minute. Most people handle that without noticing. A sizable minority get bloating, gas, cramps or loose stools, and they often conclude that they cannot tolerate protein powder at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            That conclusion is usually wrong. Purified protein is digested and absorbed efficiently in the small intestine, and very little of it reaches the large intestine, where gut bacteria produce the gas that causes bloating. What does reach the colon in quantity is carbohydrate the small intestine cannot absorb: lactose in people who do not make enough lactase, sugar alcohols such as sorbitol and maltitol, and fermentable fibers such as inulin. Those are exactly the ingredients many powders carry, often in amounts that are not obvious from the front of the tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the first job is to separate the protein from the extras. A shake that causes trouble with one brand and not another is almost always telling you something about the formula, not about whey or pea as a category. The second job is to look at the dose and the speed: even a clean powder can cause discomfort if you take a double scoop on an empty stomach and drink it in seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide is for ordinary digestive sensitivity. If your symptoms are severe, persistent, or come with warning signs such as weight loss or blood in the stool, the last section explains why that needs a doctor rather than a different tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked: Short Labels, Low Lactose, No Polyols
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run a feeding trial or a taste panel for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, published certifications and the research on lactose, FODMAPs and gut symptoms, the same method described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we mention mixing or taste, we are passing on what buyers commonly report.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every product had to clear four bars. The protein had to be an isolate, a hydrolysate, or a naturally lactose-free source such as egg white or pea, because whey concentrate carries noticeably more lactose. The label could not include sugar alcohols such as sorbitol, maltitol, xylitol or large amounts of erythritol, or prebiotic fibers such as inulin and chicory root, which are among the most common causes of shake-related bloating. Gums were allowed only in small amounts, and we flag them where they appear. And the full formula had to be disclosed, with no proprietary blends.
          </p>

          <p>
            We gave extra weight to two things. The first is formal low-FODMAP certification from Monash University or the FODMAP Friendly program, because those products have been laboratory tested at a stated serving rather than simply marketed as gentle. The second is an unsweetened or near-single-ingredient label, because a powder with almost nothing in it is the most useful tool for working out what your gut objects to.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out anything sold mainly on digestive-enzyme or probiotic blends. Some people like them, but the evidence that added enzymes change how a shake feels is thin, and they make a label harder to judge. If you want to compare purer options side by side, our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">clean protein powder guide</a> covers labels with the fewest additives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
