        <div id="culprits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Five Usual Culprits Behind Shake Bloating
          </h2>

          <p>
            When a shake causes trouble, the cause is usually one of five things. Read the ingredient list with these in mind before you give up on protein powder entirely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Lactose</h3>

          <p>
            Whey concentrate keeps some of milk's sugar, typically a few grams per serving, while isolates and hydrolysates are filtered down to around a gram or less. Research on lactose intolerance suggests many people can handle about 12 g of lactose at once, roughly a cup of milk, with mild or no symptoms, but sensitivity varies a lot, and a concentrate shake made with milk can stack the dose quickly. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate guide</a> explains the filtering in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Sugar alcohols</h3>

          <p>
            Sorbitol, maltitol, mannitol and xylitol are poorly absorbed, draw water into the gut and are fermented by bacteria, which is why they carry laxative warnings in large amounts. Erythritol is absorbed better and usually tolerated better, but big doses still cause nausea or bloating in some people. Sugar alcohols are common in low-sugar flavored powders.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Inulin and chicory root fiber</h3>

          <p>
            Inulin is a fructan, one of the most fermentable fibers there is. A few grams can cause noticeable gas in sensitive people, and it is a classic high-FODMAP ingredient. It hides under names such as chicory root fiber, oligofructose and fructooligosaccharides.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4. Gums and thickeners</h3>

          <p>
            Xanthan gum, guar gum and carrageenan give shakes body. Most people tolerate the small amounts used, but some report bloating, so a gum-free formula is worth trying if the first three are ruled out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5. The size of the serving</h3>

          <p>
            A double scoop of anything, gulped quickly with air, can cause fullness and discomfort. Before blaming an ingredient, try a single serving sipped over ten minutes.
          </p>

