        <div id="isolate-hydrolysate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Easy to Digest Protein Powder: Isolate vs Hydrolysate vs Concentrate
          </h2>

          <p>
            For whey users, the single most effective change is usually moving from concentrate to isolate. Concentrate is typically 70 to 80 percent protein by weight, with the rest made up of fat and lactose. Isolate goes through further filtering to reach 90 percent protein or more, and in the process most of the lactose goes with it. For someone with mild lactose intolerance, that difference alone often makes a shake comfortable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydrolysate goes a step further. The protein is partly broken down with enzymes into shorter peptides before it reaches you, which is why it is sometimes described as pre-digested. It is most often made from isolate, so it is also very low in lactose. Hydrolyzed proteins tend to empty from the stomach a little faster, and some people with sensitive stomachs say they feel lighter. The honest caveat is that there is little research showing hydrolysate reduces gut symptoms in healthy adults compared with a good isolate, and it costs more and often tastes more bitter.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes the order of operations simple. If a concentrate-based powder bothers you, try a plain isolate first. If an isolate with no sugar alcohols, inulin or gums still causes symptoms, a hydrolysate is a reasonable next step before you rule out whey. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate rankings</a> cover more isolates if you want options beyond the ones here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two points that confuse people. First, isolate is low in lactose, not free of milk protein. If you have a diagnosed milk allergy, isolate and hydrolysate are still unsafe unless your allergist says otherwise. Second, plenty of isolates are sold in heavily sweetened flavors with sugar alcohols or added fiber, which can undo the lactose benefit. The protein type matters, but so does the rest of the label.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fix the protein type first, then the extras.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Moving from whey concentrate to isolate solves most lactose-related bloating. If an isolate still bothers you, check for sugar alcohols, inulin and gums before paying extra for a hydrolysate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="low-fodmap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Low-FODMAP Certified Protein Powder: What the Seal Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            FODMAPs are a group of fermentable carbohydrates: fructans such as inulin, galacto-oligosaccharides found in legumes, lactose, excess fructose and polyols, the sugar alcohols. They are poorly absorbed and fermented by gut bacteria, and a low-FODMAP diet, developed by researchers at Monash University, is one of the better-studied dietary approaches for irritable bowel syndrome. It is meant to be done in phases, usually with a dietitian, rather than as a permanent way of eating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Low-FODMAP certified protein powders do exist, and they are worth knowing about if you have IBS. Two programs matter in the US market. Monash University runs its own certification and lists approved products in its app. The FODMAP Friendly program, based in Australia, also lab-tests and certifies products. In both cases the product is tested at a specific serving size, so a double scoop is not automatically covered.
          </p>

          <p>
            Among our picks, the drink wholesome unflavored egg white powder has been announced by Monash as certified low FODMAP, and Casa de Sante says its whey isolate is certified by FODMAP Friendly. Certifications can lapse or change with a reformulation, so confirm the current status in the Monash app or on the FODMAP Friendly site before you rely on it during an elimination phase.
          </p>

          <p>
            You do not strictly need a certified product. Whey isolate is generally considered low FODMAP because so little lactose remains, and an unsweetened isolate with no inulin or polyols is a reasonable choice for many people on the diet. Certification simply removes the guesswork, which is valuable when you are trying to keep an elimination phase clean.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
