        <div id="dairy-free" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Dairy Is the Problem: Egg, Pea and Other Options
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some people do not tolerate any dairy protein comfortably, even as a hydrolyzed isolate, and some have a diagnosed milk allergy, which is an immune reaction to the proteins rather than a problem digesting lactose. Either way, the answer is a non-dairy protein, and the choice among them matters more for a sensitive stomach than for most buyers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Egg white protein is the most straightforward option. It is naturally free of lactose and fiber, and single-ingredient versions have nothing else to react to. It is also a complete protein with a good amino acid profile. The drawbacks are that some egg powders are lower in protein per scoop, and egg is itself a common allergen. Our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">egg white protein powder guide</a> compares more of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pea protein is the most common plant option. Whole peas are high in galacto-oligosaccharides, which cause gas, but isolating the protein removes most of that carbohydrate. Monash has certified at least one pea protein isolate ingredient as low FODMAP, yet testing on finished pea powders is limited, and people report mixed results at large servings. Start with half a serving. Plant blends that add chickpea, lentil or other legume proteins, or inulin for fiber, are more likely to cause trouble.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rice, pumpkin seed and hemp proteins are other options, though hemp is naturally high in fiber, which some sensitive stomachs dislike. For a broader look at the non-dairy field, including blends, see our <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">best dairy-free protein powder guide</a>. And if you have a diagnosed allergy of any kind, read every label and check for cross-contact warnings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="elimination" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Find Your Trigger: A Simple Two-Week Elimination Plan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Switching from one heavily flavored powder to another rarely tells you much, because you change five variables at once. A more useful approach is to strip the shake down to almost nothing, confirm that is comfortable, and then add things back one at a time. This is general guidance, not a medical protocol; if you have IBS or another diagnosed condition, a registered dietitian can tailor it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Week one: the baseline. Use an unsweetened, unflavored isolate, such as the NOW Sports isolate on this list, or the drink wholesome egg white powder if you suspect dairy. Take a single serving a day, mixed with water, sipped over ten minutes, at the same time each day. Keep a short log of what you drank and any symptoms over the next few hours. If you are comfortable all week, you have learned that protein itself is not your problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Week two: add one variable at a time, spending two or three days on each. Try the baseline mixed with your usual milk, which tests lactose. Then try a flavored isolate with your preferred sweetener, which tests sucralose, stevia or sugar alcohols. Then try your old serving size, which tests volume. Whichever step brings the symptoms back is your likely trigger.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the baseline itself causes symptoms, try switching protein source, from whey to egg or pea or the other way round, before you conclude anything. If every clean option causes trouble, or the symptoms are significant, stop experimenting and talk to a doctor. Persistent symptoms with very plain food are information a clinician will want.
          </p>

