        <div id="how-to-drink" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Drink a Shake So It Sits Better
          </h2>

          <p>
            Even with the right powder, how you drink a shake can make the difference between comfortable and bloated. The simplest change is dose. Most people do not need more than 25 to 40 g of protein at one sitting for muscle-building purposes, and two scoops of anything can feel heavy. If you need more protein in a day, split it across two smaller shakes rather than one large one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed matters too. Drinking a shake in seconds means swallowing air along with it, and a blender or shaker bottle whips in more. Let the shake settle for a minute before drinking, then sip it over five to ten minutes. If foam bothers you, mixing with a spoon or using a little less agitation helps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to the liquid. Water is the gentlest base. Cow's milk adds lactose; lactose-free milk does not. Some plant milks contain added gums or inulin of their own, which can quietly undo a clean powder. Very cold shakes on an empty stomach bother some people, so try one at cool room temperature if you are cramping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing around exercise is worth a thought. Hard training diverts blood flow away from the gut, and a large shake right before an intense session is a common cause of discomfort. Most people find a shake easier after training or alongside a small meal. People on GLP-1 medications often find that smaller, slower shakes are the only ones they tolerate; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-glp-1/">protein powder for GLP-1 guide</a> covers that situation in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="see-a-doctor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When Bloating Is Not About the Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Occasional bloating after a shake is common and usually harmless. Persistent digestive symptoms are a different matter, and no protein powder, certified or not, is a substitute for finding out what is going on. This guide is not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            See a doctor if symptoms last for more than a few weeks, keep coming back with plain food, or come with any warning signs: unintended weight loss, blood in your stool or black stools, vomiting, difficulty swallowing, pain that wakes you at night, fever, or signs of anemia such as unusual tiredness. New, persistent bowel changes after age 45 or 50, or a family history of celiac disease, inflammatory bowel disease or colorectal cancer, also deserve prompt attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two specific points matter for protein powder users. If you suspect celiac disease, get tested before cutting out gluten, because the tests depend on you still eating it; our <a href="/best-gluten-free-protein-powder/">gluten-free protein powder guide</a> covers what to buy afterward. And if a dietitian puts you on a low-FODMAP diet, follow the full process, including the reintroduction phase, rather than staying restrictive indefinitely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone who is pregnant, has kidney disease, has a diagnosed food allergy or takes medication that affects digestion should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding regular protein shakes. A clinician can also tell you whether you need a powder at all, or whether food alone covers your protein needs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming Protein Instead of the Formula</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most shake-related bloating comes from lactose, sugar alcohols, inulin or gums, not from the protein itself. Before you give up on protein powder, read the full ingredient list and try a plain isolate or egg white powder. One clean test week will usually tell you more than a month of switching between flavored brands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying "Gut Health" Powders Loaded With Fiber</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Powders marketed for digestion often add inulin, chicory root or other prebiotic fibers, which are highly fermentable and a common cause of gas. They can suit people with robust digestion, but for a sensitive stomach they are often the worst choice on the shelf. Check for fiber sources before trusting the label claim.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Isolate Means Dairy-Free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey isolate is very low in lactose, but it is still milk protein. That makes it fine for most people with lactose intolerance and unsafe for people with a milk allergy. If an allergist has diagnosed a dairy allergy, use egg, pea or another non-dairy protein and check labels for cross-contact.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing Everything at Once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Swapping brand, flavor, liquid and serving size on the same day makes it impossible to know what helped or hurt. Start with a plain baseline, confirm it is comfortable, then change one thing every two or three days. A short symptom log turns guesswork into a clear answer within two weeks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Symptoms That Do Not Go Away</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Persistent bloating, pain or bowel changes are not something to fix by buying a different tub. If symptoms last for weeks, return with plain food, or come with weight loss, blood or night-time pain, see a doctor. Conditions such as celiac disease and IBD need diagnosis, not a supplement swap.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best protein powder for a sensitive stomach?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people it is a whey isolate or hydrolysate with no sugar alcohols, inulin or gums, such as Dymatize ISO100, because filtering removes most of the lactose. If you follow a low-FODMAP diet, a certified option like Casa de Sante whey isolate or drink wholesome egg white protein removes the guesswork. If dairy itself bothers you, choose egg white or pea.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which protein powder doesn't cause bloating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No powder is guaranteed bloat-free for everyone, but the ones least likely to cause it share a short label: an isolate, hydrolysate, egg white or pea protein, with no sugar alcohols, no chicory root or inulin, and few or no gums. Drinking one serving slowly, mixed with water, also makes a noticeable difference for many people.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey isolate easier to digest than whey concentrate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For people who are sensitive to lactose, usually yes. Isolate is filtered to about 90 percent protein or more, and most of the lactose is removed along with fat. Concentrate keeps more lactose, typically a few grams per serving. If your only issue is lactose, switching from concentrate to isolate often solves it without leaving whey behind.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are there low-FODMAP certified protein powders?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Monash University and the FODMAP Friendly program both certify protein powders after laboratory testing. Monash has announced drink wholesome's unflavored egg white powder as certified, and Casa de Sante says its whey isolate is certified by FODMAP Friendly. Certification applies at a stated serving size, so check the current listing before relying on it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can protein powder cause diarrhea?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, usually because of lactose in whey concentrate, sugar alcohols such as sorbitol or maltitol, or large amounts of added fiber, rather than the protein itself. Very large servings taken quickly can also cause loose stools. If diarrhea continues after switching to a plain isolate or egg white powder, or comes with other symptoms, see a doctor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is plant protein better for a sensitive stomach?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not automatically. Plant protein avoids lactose, which helps people who react to dairy, but many plant blends add gums, inulin or legume proteins that can cause gas. A single-ingredient pea or egg powder is a gentler non-dairy choice than a complex blend. For people who tolerate whey isolate, plant protein offers no digestive advantage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do digestive enzymes in protein powder help?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The evidence is limited. Lactase clearly helps people with lactose intolerance digest lactose, but most proprietary enzyme blends added to protein powders have little independent research showing they reduce bloating. A low-lactose isolate without sugar alcohols or inulin is a more reliable fix than an enzyme blend added to a formula that still contains common triggers.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
